> I think it is rather M2_STALK that determines this behaviour:
>
> monflag.h line 134:
>
> =A0> #define M2_STALK =A0 =A0 =A0 =A00x01000000L =A0 =A0 /* follows you t=
o other levels */
>
> =A0 There are special clauses for the Wizard of Yendor and shopkeepers -
> they keep the same behaviour even when polymorphed. Pets follow you as
> well of course.
>
> See also levl_follower() in mondata.c and =A0keepdogs() in dog.c
> (keepdogs() looks at all monsters on a level, not just pets.)
>
> 84 monsters have the M2_STALK flag, but not liches. (It's mainly various
> demons, imps, angelic beings, undead [but *no* liches] and quest nemeses
> from a quick glance.)
>
> It looks to me as though master liches and arch-liches are the only
> covetous (M3_COVETOUS or M3_WANTS[FOO]) monsters that do not have
> M2_STALK, so it's not surprising there is some confusion.
>
> Note hostile liches, along with many other hostile monsters, *will* use
> stairs to escape if you are near enough and they are low on hit points.
> Because master liches are covetous, they teleport to the upstairs when
> hurt, so they are likely to use this escape route more often than many
> other creatures. A peaceful lich has no need to escape, so it won't use
> the stairs.
>
> This is covered in find_defensive() and use_defensive() in muse.c.
>
> rogerb

